{:edb/db-before-t 1427 :edb/db-after-t 1428 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534740 50 #inst "2026-10-12T10:06:07.632Z" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186121463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121464 13194139534740 17592186045417 false] [17592186121463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121465 13194139534740 17592186045417 false] [17592186121463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121466 13194139534740 17592186045417 false] [17592186121463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121467 13194139534740 17592186045417 false] [17592186121463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121468 13194139534740 17592186045417 false] [17592186121463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434670 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186121463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434692 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186121463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434707 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186121463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434721 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186121463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434735 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186121463 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434707 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186121517 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121522 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186121523 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121525 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124570 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434754 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124570 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434755 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124570 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434759 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124570 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434764 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434758 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434762 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124647 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434756 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124647 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434757 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124647 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434761 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124647 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434763 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124647 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434765 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124648 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434751 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124648 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434752 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124648 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434753 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124648 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434760 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124811 1071 "![](images/89/897d0472039c499aee543a320f74b13bf1887f8b170ce1167a6c808d933ec676.png)\n\nThe graph $z = f(x, y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" 13194139534740 17592186045417 false] [17592186124811 1071 "![](images/89/897d0472039c499aee543a320f74b13bf1887f8b170ce1167a6c808d933ec676.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124811 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=2.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,2),$ so the global maximum point is $(1,2,2).$\n\n![](images/69/690d407982bfa9cde8fdce5b187006af1c24f9080a0cfea77e7551ca1d5cd940.png)" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124820 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x = a ∈ R^{2}$ is given by\n$f(x) \\approx - 1 + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(x - a)$.\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $f^{″}(a)$ are $λ_{1} = 3 - \\sqrt{5}$ and $λ_{2} = 3 + \\sqrt{5}$, what type of point is $a$?" 13194139534740 17592186045417 false] [17592186124820 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3-\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3+\\sqrt{5},$ what type of point is $a?$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124820 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =3-\\sqrt{5}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =3+\\sqrt{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124821 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124821 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x = a ∈ R^{2}$ is given by\n$f(x) \\approx - 12 + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}A(x - a)$,\n\nwhere\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11} =-2$ and $det(A) =-24$\n2. $A$ is positive-definite\n3. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 false] [17592186124821 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-12+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-24$\n\nII. $A$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186124821 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-2$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}|=-24.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}|=-24<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434656 1080 "saddle point" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434656 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434656 1083 #uuid "002ed759-21f8-5564-aa23-e5ae38d108b5" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434657 1080 "$B$ and $D$ only" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434657 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434657 1083 #uuid "04a2ce01-d899-5e97-a2f6-d48bd4f3ca01" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434658 1080 "$C$ only" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434658 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434658 1083 #uuid "0f25997d-4b61-53ec-9106-4119ee276f82" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434659 1080 "local minimum" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434659 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434659 1083 #uuid "1342b725-dc31-5b5a-99d1-77316495d1ef" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434660 1080 "I and III only" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434660 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434660 1083 #uuid "1344f994-c7f3-5ec5-8bc3-dd5fb9f3b026" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434661 1080 "I and II only" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434661 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434661 1083 #uuid "1b15bd6b-53d9-5f2c-a622-474fbfa83e12" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434662 1080 "$D$ only" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434662 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434662 1083 #uuid "1d15721b-98b5-5633-b7d7-1d495541a3bb" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434663 1080 "$C$ only" 13194139534740 17592186045417 tr