 $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-78+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6,{\\lambda }_{2}=2-2\\sqrt{13}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2+2\\sqrt{13},$ what type of point is $a?$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434751 1072 #uuid "2a2b77d8-21b3-512a-8f60-975fa8c9b29e" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434751 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-6<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =2-2\\sqrt{13}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =2+2\\sqrt{13}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434742 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434752 1069 "q-163776" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434752 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434752 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1+\\sqrt{5},$ what type of point is $a?$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434752 1072 #uuid "463ee037-d37e-5536-bfb1-df9c529010e2" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434752 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both negative and positive eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =1-\\sqrt{5}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =1+\\sqrt{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434741 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434753 1069 "q-163772" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434753 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434753 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ what type of point is $a?$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434753 1072 #uuid "4c7c764b-5efc-585f-b1d6-ab32ef1c26ef" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434753 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =1>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434754 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434746 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434754 1069 "q-163781" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434754 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434754 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈5+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=8$\n\nII. $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local maximum of $f$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434754 1072 #uuid "5ec0f059-837e-5e3d-9bb4-8d292f2af4e9" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434754 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-3$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|=8.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-3<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|=8>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434755 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434740 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434755 1069 "q-163786" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434755 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434755 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=3$\n\nII. $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a saddle point of $f$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434755 1072 #uuid "65b2f5b4-89e0-551f-a08f-1b697ac55d76" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434755 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(2)-1(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434756 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434747 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434756 1069 "q-164151" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434756 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434756 1071 "![](images/f7/f7429a2fc291fc9e14685208288f670cca341a260ac13b358c52d99c35e13b4f.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434756 1072 #uuid "73e92678-31f3-525b-9096-a06391739cab" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434756 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $D.$ Hence, $A$ and $D$ are local minima.\n\n![](images/b8/b8952aa59c9351c9723cd62f35073d46d7ddfd46afa9a9b3ddae7ce149065c59.png)" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434757 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434749 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434757 1069 "q-164168" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434757 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434757 1071 "![](images/f3/f3852805b0ae44433828113396fa63d0688ec06ae57886428815515e521baa49.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434757 1072 #uuid "7f6e52f3-8502-516e-a6ab-c65771746796" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434757 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in 