by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434748 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434760 1069 "q-163774" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434760 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1+\\sqrt{26}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1-\\sqrt{26},$ what type of point is $a?$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434760 1072 #uuid "ae219904-d456-5249-bf4b-05599f3e75b5" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434760 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both negative and positive eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1+\\sqrt{26}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-1-\\sqrt{26}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434737 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434761 1069 "q-164153" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434761 1071 "![](images/79/79971602dd6834147c3db9361c94853a5a66c8e899978df3629d2841326fe62c.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434761 1072 #uuid "c719ba47-2cb2-55e7-85ae-fa0a3ade984e" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434761 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhood exists for the point $B.$ Hence, $B$ is a local maximum.\n\n![](images/68/685c03b86db93032c8a79334c5fa46c561dd263a9aa4f52b9d7e6f41c3bb827c.png)" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434762 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434736 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434762 1069 "q-163835" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434762 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434762 1071 "![](images/58/58898653e07df36c7de1eac79aa9ce012e871c5323a344381645333eb1695adb.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global maximum of the function?" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434762 1072 #uuid "e313337f-f7bb-58d5-96bd-6538b4821d8f" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434762 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=1.5.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $1.5.$ The function reaches this value at both $(x,y)=(0,-2)$ and $(x,y)=(-2,1).$\n\n![](images/4b/4b8b56801adbffd5dd65b6e110593fdc0141b70315306c67ec7ed5842ae7f7d9.png)" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434763 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434750 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434763 1069 "q-164101" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434763 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434763 1071 "![](images/f4/f4ca398cfb365346962fc66ffecd7fb85390a9a98c49cb1926f6d8093a01c8bc.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434763 1072 #uuid "e433d738-c2a7-5a16-852b-741bb762e1fe" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434763 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence, $A$ and $C$ are local minima.\n\n![](images/02/0262a775dad79697ea888b6047e607c12ec804dbb734b72b2ca8fda54d6c130f.png)" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434764 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434744 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434764 1069 "q-163788" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434764 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434764 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=-15$\n\nII. $A$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434764 1072 #uuid "e5518b1c-8ee2-55b4-900e-3e5d5d3b8c0e" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434764 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1(3)-4(4)+2(-6) \\\\  & =-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-19<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-31<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434738 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434765 1069 "q-164154" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434765 1071 "![](images/81/81604581fae8b25776727eca4450caa7d48c42a781706455ed585d5cfc4a25fa.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434765 1072 #uuid "e9cc186b-22af-55d8-bf89-1dd19ae7b90f" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true] [17592186434765 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence $A$ and $C$ are local maximum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $A({x}_{A},{y}_{A},{z}_{A}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{A},{y}_{A})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $A$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](images/36/3651b43178819666002775a26071a0bd2c9cb48b58a3c5516c38e085d50fc4a6.png)" 13194139534740 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-002ed759-21f8-5564-aa23-e5ae38d108b5" 17592186434656 "answer-04a2ce01-d899-5e97-a2f6-d48bd4f3ca01" 17592186434657 "answer-0f25997d-4b61-53ec-9106-4119ee276f82" 17592186434658 "answer-1342b725-dc31-5b5a-99d1-77316495d1ef" 17592186434659 "answer-1344f994-c7f3-5e